insert into public.flashcards (id, cert, deck, domain, objective, front, back) values
('cy-f-300','cysa','core',2,'2.1',$$What is asset discovery scanning and why does it precede vulnerability scanning?$$,$$A sweep (ping sweep, ARP scan, service enumeration) that identifies which hosts and services are actually live on the network before assessing them for flaws, so the vulnerability scan targets real, in-scope systems instead of an assumed or outdated list.$$),
('cy-f-301','cysa','core',2,'2.1',$$What does OS/service fingerprinting during a scan determine, and why does it matter?$$,$$Identifying the operating system, service, and version running on a target from banners and protocol responses, so the scanner can select only the checks relevant to that platform - improving both accuracy and speed.$$),
('cy-f-302','cysa','core',2,'2.1',$$How do internal and external vulnerability scans differ in perspective?$$,$$External scans probe from outside the network boundary, showing what an internet-based attacker could see and reach; internal scans run from inside, revealing what could be exploited once someone (an insider or a foothold) is already past the perimeter - both are needed for full coverage.$$),
('cy-f-303','cysa','core',2,'2.1',$$What is the difference between agent-based and agentless vulnerability scanning?$$,$$Agent-based scanning installs lightweight software on each host that reports continuously, working well for remote or cloud assets not always on a scannable segment; agentless scanning probes over the network from a central scanner with nothing installed on the target, but is blind to devices that are off-network at scan time.$$),
('cy-f-304','cysa','core',2,'2.1',$$What is a scan scope agreement and why must it be settled before running a vulnerability scan?$$,$$A documented, pre-agreed definition of exactly which IP ranges, systems, and time windows are in scope, and which are explicitly excluded, preventing an authorized scan from unexpectedly touching production systems or running outside what was approved.$$),
('cy-f-305','cysa','core',2,'2.1',$$What does PCI DSS require regarding external vulnerability scanning?$$,$$Merchants handling cardholder data must undergo external vulnerability scans at least quarterly, and after significant network changes, performed by a PCI SSC-certified Approved Scanning Vendor (ASV).$$),
('cy-f-306','cysa','core',2,'2.1',$$What factors typically drive how often an organization schedules vulnerability scans?$$,$$Regulatory mandates, asset/data criticality, how rapidly the environment changes, and the organization's risk appetite together set cadence - critical or fast-changing environments are scanned far more often than static, low-value ones.$$),
('cy-f-307','cysa','core',2,'2.1',$$How does network segmentation affect vulnerability-scan coverage and planning?$$,$$A scanner deployed in one segment generally cannot reach hosts in an isolated segment without a local scan engine, jump host, or credentialed agent placed inside that zone, so segmented networks need scanning planned per zone or blind spots will go undetected.$$),
('cy-f-308','cysa','core',2,'2.1',$$Why does the sensitivity of the data on a system influence how it is scanned?$$,$$Systems holding highly sensitive or regulated data often warrant more cautious, carefully scheduled scanning and stricter access controls around the results themselves, since a scan report is effectively a catalog of that system's exploitable weaknesses.$$),
('cy-f-309','cysa','core',2,'2.2',$$What key fields does a typical vulnerability scan finding include, and why?$$,$$The affected host/IP, a specific plugin or CVE identifier, a severity rating, a description of the issue, and a remediation recommendation - together letting an analyst triage the finding without needing to re-research it from scratch.$$),
('cy-f-310','cysa','core',2,'2.2',$$Why do analysts group scan findings by CVE or plugin ID across many hosts instead of triaging host by host?$$,$$Grouping reveals that one root-cause vulnerability affects dozens or hundreds of hosts, letting a single fix - one patch or configuration change - close many findings at once instead of treating each host as an unrelated case.$$),
('cy-f-311','cysa','core',2,'2.2',$$What is scan-to-scan trending used for when analyzing vulnerability-scan-tool output?$$,$$Comparing successive scans of the same environment to see which findings are new, which were remediated, and which persist unresolved across cycles - revealing whether the vulnerability management program is actually making progress over time.$$),
('cy-f-312','cysa','core',2,'2.2',$$How does a web application scanner''s output typically differ from an infrastructure/network scanner''s output?$$,$$A web app scanner reports findings tied to a specific URL and parameter with request/response evidence, such as an injection point; an infrastructure scanner reports host/service-level findings tied to an IP, open port, and installed software version.$$),
('cy-f-313','cysa','core',2,'2.2',$$What does a cloud infrastructure assessment tool typically flag that a traditional network scanner would not?$$,$$Misconfigurations in cloud-native resources - an overly permissive storage bucket policy, an exposed API key, an unencrypted database, or excessive IAM permissions - issues defined by configuration rather than a missing patch.$$),
('cy-f-314','cysa','core',2,'2.2',$$What does it mean when a scan report labels one finding "confirmed" and a similar finding elsewhere "potential"?$$,$$A "confirmed" finding was actively verified, such as by a successful check or a credentialed version match; a "potential" finding only matched an indirect signal, like banner text, and needs manual confirmation before remediation resources are committed to it.$$),
('cy-f-315','cysa','core',2,'2.2',$$Why is normalizing output from multiple different scanning tools into one common format important?$$,$$Different scanners use their own severity scales, ID schemes, and report layouts; normalizing everything to a common reference like CVE/CVSS lets an analyst see one unified risk picture instead of manually reconciling incompatible reports.$$),
('cy-f-316','cysa','core',2,'2.3',$$Besides CVSS severity, what other criteria should feed vulnerability prioritization decisions?$$,$$Asset/data criticality, whether the system is exposed to attack (internet-facing versus isolated), real-world exploitability (public exploit code, active campaigns), and the financial/reputational value at risk if the asset is compromised.$$),
('cy-f-317','cysa','core',2,'2.3',$$What is the difference between a proof-of-concept exploit and a weaponized exploit, and why does it matter for prioritization?$$,$$A proof-of-concept demonstrates a flaw is exploitable but often requires real expertise to use; a weaponized exploit is packaged into ready-to-use tooling that lowers the skill bar dramatically - once weaponized, a vulnerability's real-world risk jumps sharply and prioritization should follow.$$),
('cy-f-318','cysa','core',2,'2.3',$$How does an organization''s risk appetite influence vulnerability prioritization thresholds?$$,$$A risk-averse organization sets stricter remediation SLAs and lower severity thresholds for urgent action; a more risk-tolerant one may accept longer timelines for lower-severity findings - the same finding can be prioritized differently at two organizations with different appetites.$$),
('cy-f-319','cysa','core',2,'2.3',$$Why does public availability of exploit code raise a vulnerability''s priority even though its CVSS score doesn''t change?$$,$$CVSS measures intrinsic technical severity, not how easily an attacker can currently use the flaw; once working exploit code circulates publicly, practical exploitation likelihood rises sharply even though the CVSS number stays fixed.$$),
('cy-f-320','cysa','core',2,'2.3',$$What is "value at risk" in vulnerability prioritization, and what two dimensions does it usually combine?$$,$$An estimate of what a successful exploit would cost the organization, typically combining financial impact (direct loss, fines, recovery cost) and reputational impact (customer trust, brand damage) for the specific asset affected.$$),
('cy-f-321','cysa','core',2,'2.3',$$What role does cybersecurity insurance play in vulnerability prioritization?$$,$$Insurers often set minimum security requirements, such as patch timelines or MFA, as a condition of coverage; failing to meet them can push certain vulnerabilities higher in priority to avoid a denied claim or policy cancellation after an incident.$$),
('cy-f-322','cysa','core',2,'2.3',$$What is SSVC (Stakeholder-Specific Vulnerability Categorization) as a prioritization approach?$$,$$A decision-tree-based framework that categorizes a vulnerability's urgency using structured factors - exploitation status, exposure, mission impact - tailored to a specific organization's context, producing an action recommendation rather than only a numeric score.$$),
('cy-f-323','cysa','core',2,'2.3',$$Why can a vulnerability with no known public exploit still warrant high prioritization?$$,$$If it sits on a mission-critical, internet-facing, or highly sensitive asset, exposure and potential impact alone can justify urgent treatment - waiting for exploit code to appear before acting is a reactive, not risk-based, posture.$$),
('cy-f-324','cysa','core',2,'2.3',$$What is vulnerability triage, and how does it differ from remediation itself?$$,$$Triage is the analysis step that sorts and ranks newly discovered vulnerabilities by risk to decide what gets worked on first and by whom; remediation is the actual fixing that happens afterward, guided by triage's output.$$),
('cy-f-325','cysa','core',2,'2.4',$$What is input validation and why is it considered a foundational control against injection-style vulnerabilities?$$,$$Checking that user-supplied data matches expected type, length, format, and range before it is processed; most injection attacks rely on unexpected input reaching a sensitive function, so rejecting malformed input at the boundary closes off the entry point.$$),
('cy-f-326','cysa','core',2,'2.4',$$How do parameterized queries (prepared statements) prevent SQL injection?$$,$$They separate the SQL command structure from the data supplied by the user, so attacker input is always treated strictly as data - never as executable query syntax - regardless of what characters it contains.$$),
('cy-f-327','cysa','core',2,'2.4',$$What is output encoding and which vulnerability class does it primarily mitigate?$$,$$Converting data into a safe representation, such as escaping HTML special characters, before rendering it back to a browser, so injected script in user input displays as inert text instead of executing - the primary defense against cross-site scripting.$$),
('cy-f-328','cysa','core',2,'2.4',$$What is a buffer overflow vulnerability and what controls mitigate it?$$,$$Writing more data into a fixed-size memory buffer than it can hold, corrupting adjacent memory and potentially enabling code execution; mitigated by bounds checking, safe string-handling functions, memory-safe languages, and OS-level protections like ASLR and stack canaries.$$),
('cy-f-329','cysa','core',2,'2.4',$$What is a use-after-free vulnerability?$$,$$A memory-safety flaw where a program continues using a pointer to memory that has already been freed; an attacker can exploit it by reallocating that memory with attacker-controlled data, potentially leading to code execution.$$),
('cy-f-330','cysa','core',2,'2.4',$$What is command injection and how does its root cause compare to SQL injection?$$,$$Command injection tricks an application into passing attacker-supplied input to the underlying operating system shell for execution rather than into a database query; the target differs, but the root cause - unsanitized input reaching an interpreter - is the same pattern.$$),
('cy-f-331','cysa','core',2,'2.4',$$What are common cryptographic-failure vulnerabilities a control review should catch?$$,$$Use of outdated or weak algorithms (MD5, SHA-1, DES), hardcoded encryption keys, missing encryption of sensitive data at rest or in transit, and improperly validated TLS certificates - each undermines the protection cryptography was supposed to provide.$$),
('cy-f-332','cysa','core',2,'2.4',$$What is broken authentication and what controls mitigate it?$$,$$Weaknesses like missing account lockout, predictable session tokens, or credentials handled insecurely that let an attacker impersonate a legitimate user; mitigated with MFA, secure session management, rate limiting, and strong credential handling.$$),
('cy-f-333','cysa','core',2,'2.4',$$What is a Web Application Firewall (WAF) and what does it protect against?$$,$$An inline or reverse-proxy control that inspects HTTP(S) traffic against rulesets for known attack patterns, such as SQL injection and XSS, blocking malicious requests before they reach the application - a compensating control in front of the app, not a fix for the underlying code.$$),
('cy-f-334','cysa','core',2,'2.4',$$What is the principle of least privilege as a control against software vulnerability impact?$$,$$Granting a user, process, or service only the minimum access needed to perform its function, so that if a vulnerability is exploited, the resulting compromise is limited to what that narrow set of privileges can reach.$$),
('cy-f-335','cysa','core',2,'2.4',$$Why does secure-coding training act earlier in the defense chain than a control like a WAF?$$,$$Secure-coding training teaches developers to avoid introducing vulnerability classes during development itself, preventing the flaw from ever existing in the code, rather than relying on a runtime control to catch it after the fact.$$),
('cy-f-336','cysa','core',2,'2.4',$$What is RASP (Runtime Application Self-Protection) and how does it differ from a WAF?$$,$$A control embedded inside the running application itself that monitors its own execution and can block an attack in real time using actual application context, unlike a WAF, which sits outside the application inspecting network traffic without that internal visibility.$$),
('cy-f-337','cysa','core',2,'2.4',$$What is a hardening baseline, such as a CIS Benchmark, and how does it reduce attack surface?$$,$$A documented, vetted set of secure configuration settings for an OS or application - disabling unnecessary services, enforcing strong defaults - applied so a system has fewer exploitable settings and services present from the start.$$),
('cy-f-338','cysa','core',2,'2.4',$$Why is network segmentation considered a compensating control against a software vulnerability that can''t be immediately patched?$$,$$It doesn't fix the flaw itself, but it limits which systems can reach the vulnerable one and what an attacker could pivot to if they exploited it - reducing the practical blast radius while a real fix is pending.$$),
('cy-f-339','cysa','core',2,'2.5',$$What does a vulnerability management policy typically define at the governance level?$$,$$Roles and responsibilities, scanning cadence requirements, remediation SLA tiers by severity, the exception/risk-acceptance process, and escalation paths - the rules the whole program operates under, distinct from any single scan or fix.$$),
('cy-f-340','cysa','core',2,'2.5',$$What is a patch management pipeline''s typical sequence before a fix reaches production?$$,$$Testing the patch in a non-production/staging environment, scheduling a change-controlled deployment window, applying the patch to production, and validating success with a rollback plan ready in case the patch causes issues.$$),
('cy-f-341','cysa','core',2,'2.5',$$What is a vulnerability exception (waiver) process, and why must exceptions expire?$$,$$A formal, time-bound approval to leave a specific finding unremediated for a documented reason, with compensating controls where possible; giving it an expiration forces periodic reassessment instead of letting an old exception silently become permanent as conditions change.$$),
('cy-f-342','cysa','core',2,'2.5',$$What does an escalation procedure in vulnerability management define?$$,$$The trigger conditions - such as an SLA breach or a critical finding on a crown-jewel asset - and the chain of people who must be notified and involved when normal remediation isn't happening fast enough on its own.$$),
('cy-f-343','cysa','core',2,'2.5',$$What is Coordinated Vulnerability Disclosure (CVD) and what does the embargo period accomplish?$$,$$A process where a researcher privately reports a flaw to the affected vendor, who is given a defined window - the embargo - to develop and release a fix before the details are published, balancing the researcher's right to disclose against giving defenders time to patch first.$$),
('cy-f-344','cysa','core',2,'2.5',$$What is a PSIRT (Product Security Incident Response Team) and how does it relate to coordinated disclosure?$$,$$A vendor's dedicated team that receives, triages, and coordinates the fix and disclosure timeline for vulnerabilities reported in that vendor's own products - the internal counterpart that makes coordinated disclosure actually function.$$),
('cy-f-345','cysa','core',2,'2.5',$$Why does end-of-life (EOL) software represent an ongoing vulnerability management challenge rather than a one-time finding?$$,$$Once a vendor stops issuing patches, every newly discovered flaw in that software remains permanently unremediated by the vendor, forcing the organization into ongoing compensating controls, such as isolation and monitoring, or a costly migration rather than a simple patch.$$),
('cy-f-346','cysa','core',2,'2.5',$$What is continuous vulnerability management, as opposed to periodic point-in-time scanning?$$,$$An ongoing process of near-real-time asset discovery, scanning, and prioritization integrated into daily operations, rather than a scheduled quarterly or monthly snapshot - better suited to fast-changing cloud and DevOps environments.$$),
('cy-f-347','cysa','core',2,'2.5',$$Why is asset inventory integration essential to effective vulnerability response?$$,$$You cannot remediate, prioritize by criticality, or even know a finding matters if you don't know what the affected asset is, who owns it, and how critical it is - an accurate, current inventory is a prerequisite for every downstream response decision.$$),
('cy-f-348','cysa','core',2,'2.5',$$What is root cause analysis applied to vulnerability management, and what does it aim to prevent?$$,$$Investigating why a vulnerability, or a whole class of them, existed in the first place - a missing process step, a bad default image, absent code review - so the underlying gap is fixed, preventing the same category of finding from recurring repeatedly.$$),
('cy-f-349','cysa','core',2,'2.5',$$What is change management''s role in vulnerability remediation, and what tension does it create?$$,$$Change management requires testing, approval, and scheduling before a fix reaches production, protecting stability - but this same process can slow urgent remediation, requiring an expedited/emergency-change path for critical, actively-exploited findings.$$),
('cy-f-350','cysa','core',2,'2.5',$$Why does the exact starting point of a remediation SLA clock matter, and what problem arises if teams define it inconsistently?$$,$$Whether the SLA clock starts at discovery, at disclosure, or at fix validation significantly affects measured performance; if teams define this differently, their SLA-compliance percentages aren't actually comparable and can misrepresent how the program is really performing.$$),
('cy-a-120','cysa','acronym',2,'2.1',$$ASV$$,$$Approved Scanning Vendor - a PCI SSC-certified vendor authorized to perform the quarterly external vulnerability scans required for PCI DSS compliance.$$),
('cy-a-121','cysa','acronym',2,'2.1',$$OVAL$$,$$Open Vulnerability and Assessment Language - a SCAP component standard for describing system state (vulnerabilities, configuration, patches) as machine-readable, testable definitions.$$),
('cy-a-122','cysa','acronym',2,'2.1',$$XCCDF$$,$$Extensible Configuration Checklist Description Format - the SCAP component standard for writing structured, automatable security checklists and benchmarks.$$),
('cy-a-123','cysa','acronym',2,'2.1',$$NVD$$,$$National Vulnerability Database - the U.S. government repository that publishes standardized, machine-readable CVE and CVSS data referenced by most scanners.$$),
('cy-a-124','cysa','acronym',2,'2.2',$$SARIF$$,$$Static Analysis Results Interchange Format - a standardized JSON format for the output of static analysis and scanning tools, letting different scanners feed a common pipeline and dashboard.$$),
('cy-a-125','cysa','acronym',2,'2.2',$$CNAPP$$,$$Cloud-Native Application Protection Platform - a class of tool combining cloud posture management, workload scanning, and application/API protection into one output feed for cloud environments.$$),
('cy-a-126','cysa','acronym',2,'2.2',$$EASM$$,$$External Attack Surface Management - continuous scanning and discovery of an organization's internet-facing assets from an outsider's perspective, surfacing unknown or forgotten exposed systems.$$),
('cy-a-127','cysa','acronym',2,'2.2',$$CAASM$$,$$Cyber Asset Attack Surface Management - aggregates data from multiple existing tools (scanners, CMDB, cloud inventories) to produce a unified, deduplicated asset and exposure view.$$),
('cy-a-128','cysa','acronym',2,'2.3',$$SSVC$$,$$Stakeholder-Specific Vulnerability Categorization - a decision-tree prioritization framework that factors exploitation status, exposure, and mission impact into an action recommendation tailored to the organization.$$),
('cy-a-129','cysa','acronym',2,'2.3',$$PoC$$,$$Proof of Concept - in vulnerability prioritization, a demonstration exploit showing a flaw is genuinely exploitable, distinct from a fully weaponized, ready-to-use exploit tool.$$),
('cy-a-130','cysa','acronym',2,'2.3',$$RBVM$$,$$Risk-Based Vulnerability Management - a prioritization approach that ranks vulnerabilities by real-world risk (exploitability, exposure, asset value) rather than by severity score alone.$$),
('cy-a-131','cysa','acronym',2,'2.3',$$ALE$$,$$Annualized Loss Expectancy - the expected yearly monetary loss from a risk, calculated by multiplying the cost of a single occurrence by its estimated annual rate of occurrence, used to weigh value at risk.$$),
('cy-a-132','cysa','acronym',2,'2.4',$$WAF$$,$$Web Application Firewall - inspects HTTP(S) traffic against rulesets to block known attack patterns like SQL injection and cross-site scripting before they reach the application.$$),
('cy-a-133','cysa','acronym',2,'2.4',$$XSS$$,$$Cross-Site Scripting - injecting malicious script into content viewed by other users' browsers, mitigated primarily through output encoding and a content security policy.$$),
('cy-a-134','cysa','acronym',2,'2.4',$$CSRF$$,$$Cross-Site Request Forgery - tricking an authenticated user's browser into submitting an unwanted request, mitigated with anti-CSRF tokens and same-site cookie settings.$$),
('cy-a-135','cysa','acronym',2,'2.4',$$XXE$$,$$XML External Entity injection - exploiting an XML parser that resolves external entity references to read local files or perform SSRF, mitigated by disabling external entity resolution in the parser.$$),
('cy-a-136','cysa','acronym',2,'2.4',$$RASP$$,$$Runtime Application Self-Protection - a control embedded within a running application that monitors and blocks attacks in real time using internal application context.$$),
('cy-a-137','cysa','acronym',2,'2.5',$$CVD$$,$$Coordinated Vulnerability Disclosure - a process where a researcher privately reports a flaw to the vendor, who is given a defined embargo period to fix it before public disclosure.$$),
('cy-a-138','cysa','acronym',2,'2.5',$$EOL$$,$$End of Life - the point at which a vendor stops releasing patches for a product, leaving any future vulnerabilities in it permanently unremediated by the vendor.$$),
('cy-a-139','cysa','acronym',2,'2.5',$$PSIRT$$,$$Product Security Incident Response Team - a vendor's dedicated team that receives, triages, and coordinates fixes and disclosure timelines for vulnerabilities found in its own products.$$),
('cy-y-060','cysa','feynman',2,'2.1',$$Explain to a new hire why an organization runs both internal and external vulnerability scans instead of just one.$$,$$Model answer: an external scan shows what an anonymous attacker on the internet could see and reach - the outside perimeter view. But most real intrusions eventually involve something happening inside the network too, whether from a compromised laptop, an insider, or a foothold gained through phishing. Internal scans show that side: what could be reached and exploited once someone, or something, is already past the perimeter. Relying on only one gives a false sense of security about the half you never actually checked.$$),
('cy-y-061','cysa','feynman',2,'2.1',$$Explain why network segmentation, which improves security, can complicate a vulnerability scanning program.$$,$$Model answer: segmentation is great for containment - it stops one compromised segment from directly reaching another. But that same isolation means a scanner sitting in one segment often can't see hosts in another without deliberately being given a path in, such as a local scan engine, jump host, or credentialed agent inside each zone. If a team just points one central scanner at everything, they'll silently miss whatever segmentation is blocking, so scan architecture has to be planned zone by zone, not aimed at one flat network.$$),
('cy-y-062','cysa','feynman',2,'2.2',$$Explain to a new analyst why they shouldn''t act on a raw scan report without first grouping and normalizing the findings.$$,$$Model answer: a raw report from one scanner lists every finding on every host separately, in that vendor's own severity language, so 200 hosts affected by the same root-cause flaw look like 200 unrelated problems. Group by CVE or plugin ID first to see it's really one fix. If you're also combining output from a web scanner and a cloud posture tool, normalize their different severity scales into one common view, like CVSS, otherwise you'll misjudge relative priority by comparing apples to oranges.$$),
('cy-y-063','cysa','feynman',3,'2.2',$$Explain how you''d use scan-to-scan trending to tell your manager whether the vulnerability program is actually working, not just producing reports.$$,$$Model answer: pull the last several scan cycles and compare them - how many new findings appeared each cycle, how many previously reported findings are now gone and confirmed fixed by a clean rescan, and how many keep reappearing unresolved cycle after cycle. If the fixed count consistently outpaces new findings and few items are stuck, the program is working. If the same findings just keep showing up scan after scan, the reports are being produced but nothing is closing the loop.$$),
('cy-y-064','cysa','feynman',2,'2.3',$$Explain to a manager why two vulnerabilities with the exact same CVSS score of 9.8 might get very different remediation deadlines on your team''s priority list.$$,$$Model answer: CVSS 9.8 says both are technically about equally severe if exploited, but that's not the whole risk picture. If one sits on an internet-facing system holding customer payment data and already has a public exploit circulating, and the other sits on an isolated internal test box with no real exploit activity, the first is a live emergency and the second isn't, despite identical scores. Real prioritization layers exposure, exploitability, and asset value on top of severity, so the deadlines should differ even though the CVSS numbers don't.$$),
('cy-y-065','cysa','feynman',3,'2.3',$$Explain SSVC to someone who''s used to a simple CVSS-based priority list and thinks it''s redundant.$$,$$Model answer: a CVSS list gives you one number to sort by, but it can't tell you "should we patch this today or this quarter" - it just says how bad it could theoretically be. SSVC instead walks through a decision tree asking things like: is this already being exploited, is the affected system exposed, would a compromise actually hurt our mission-critical operations. It converts those answers into a concrete action, like "act now" versus "schedule normally", which is a decision, not just a score, and it's tailored to your organization instead of being generic.$$),
('cy-y-066','cysa','feynman',2,'2.4',$$Explain to a junior developer why parameterized queries stop SQL injection even if you never explicitly filter out characters like quotes.$$,$$Model answer: the trick isn't filtering out dangerous characters - it's changing how the database interprets the input at all. A parameterized query sends the SQL command structure and the user's data as two separate things; the database engine builds the query first with placeholders, then plugs in the data afterward as pure data, never as part of the command itself. So even if someone types a quote or a semicolon, it's still just a string value being inserted, not new SQL syntax - there's nothing to escape because the data can never become code in the first place.$$),
('cy-y-067','cysa','feynman',2,'2.4',$$Explain the difference between a WAF and RASP to someone who thinks they''re the same thing, using a building-security analogy.$$,$$Model answer: a WAF is like a guard at the building's front door, checking everyone entering against a list of known troublemakers and suspicious behavior - it only sees what happens at that entry point, the network traffic, and has no idea what happens once someone's inside. RASP is like a security system built into every room of the building itself, watching what's actually happening based on real context - if someone tries to open a vault they were never authorized to reach, it reacts at that exact moment, using information a front-door guard could never have. A WAF protects the perimeter; RASP protects from within the application's own execution.$$),
('cy-y-068','cysa','feynman',2,'2.5',$$Explain to a colleague why an unpatchable, end-of-life system needs an ongoing management plan instead of a single ticket that eventually gets closed.$$,$$Model answer: normally a vulnerability finding is a temporary state - you patch it, rescan, and close the ticket. But once a vendor stops supporting a product, every new flaw discovered in it from now on will never get an official patch. Closing the ticket once doesn't make that go away; next month's scan will just find the next unpatched issue in the same dead product. The real fix has to be an ongoing plan - isolate it, monitor it closely, and set a real migration timeline - not a single finding you check off.$$),
('cy-y-069','cysa','feynman',2,'2.5',$$Explain coordinated vulnerability disclosure to a researcher who wants to publish full exploit details the same day they find a bug.$$,$$Model answer: publishing immediately feels honest, but it also hands attackers a working blueprint before the vendor has had any chance to fix it - everyone using that product becomes exposed the moment it's public, defenders included. Coordinated disclosure asks the researcher to report privately to the vendor first and agree to an embargo period, giving the vendor time to build and ship a patch. The researcher still gets credit and can still publish once the fix is out or the embargo expires - the only thing that changes is the order, so the fix arrives before the details do, not after.$$);
insert into public.questions (id, cert, domain, objective, qtype, difficulty, stem, choices, answer, explanation) values
('cy-q-300','cysa',2,'2.1','mcq',1,$q$Before an organization runs its first vulnerability scan against a new environment, the security team performs a network sweep to identify every live host and open service. What is this preliminary step called, and why is it performed first?$q$,$q$["Asset discovery scanning, so the vulnerability scan actually targets real, in-scope systems instead of an assumed list","Penetration testing, to exploit systems before assessing them","Patch management, to apply fixes ahead of the scan","Log correlation, to review historical alerts"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Asset discovery identifies live hosts and services first, ensuring the vulnerability scan that follows has an accurate, complete target list. Skipping it risks scanning against a stale or incomplete inventory. Penetration testing, patching, and log correlation are unrelated preliminary steps.$q$),
('cy-q-301','cysa',2,'2.1','mcq',2,$q$A vulnerability scanner examines a target's response banners and TCP/IP stack behavior to determine it is running a specific Linux distribution and kernel version before selecting which checks to run. What is this technique called?$q$,$q$["OS/service fingerprinting","Credentialed authentication","Passive traffic capture","Compliance scanning"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Fingerprinting identifies the operating system and service versions from observable responses, letting the scanner select only the checks relevant to that platform rather than running every possible check against every host, which improves both accuracy and speed.$q$),
('cy-q-302','cysa',2,'2.1','mcq',2,$q$A security team runs one scan from a host on the public internet against the company's edge, and a separate scan from a workstation inside the corporate LAN. What is the PRIMARY reason for running both?$q$,$q$["External scanning shows what an outside attacker could reach; internal scanning shows what could be exploited from inside the perimeter or by an insider","Internal and external scans always report identical results, so running both is redundant","External scanning has been deprecated in favor of internal scanning","Running both wastes resources since one perspective is always sufficient"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$External and internal scans represent genuinely different attacker perspectives - what's reachable from outside the network boundary versus what's reachable once inside it - so each surfaces exposures the other cannot, and neither alone gives full coverage.$q$),
('cy-q-303','cysa',2,'2.1','multi',2,$q$A security team is deciding between agent-based and agentless vulnerability scanning for a fleet that includes many remote laptops rarely connected to the corporate network. Select the TWO accurate statements. (Choose two.)$q$,$q$["An installed agent can report on a laptop's posture even when it is off the corporate network, as long as it has internet access","Agentless scanning from a central scanner will reliably assess laptops that are never connected to a scannable network segment","Agent-based scanning requires software to be deployed and maintained on each endpoint","Agentless scanning requires installing persistent software on every target host"]$q$::jsonb,$q$[0,2]$q$::jsonb,$q$Agent-based scanning reports continuously regardless of network location, since the agent itself sends data out, but it does require deploying and maintaining software on every endpoint. Agentless scanning probes over the network and has nothing installed on the target, so it cannot assess a device that's never on a reachable segment - the opposite of what the second and fourth options claim.$q$),
('cy-q-304','cysa',2,'2.1','mcq',1,$q$Prior to running an authorized vulnerability scan against a client's network, the assessment team and the client sign a document defining exactly which IP ranges are in scope, which systems are explicitly excluded, and the scan window. What does this document primarily prevent?$q$,$q$["The scanner from being detected by an IDS","The scan from unexpectedly touching systems or running outside what was authorized","The need for a credentialed scan account","The vulnerability report from containing false positives"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$A documented scan scope agreement exists precisely to prevent an authorized scan from drifting outside its approved boundaries - hitting excluded systems or running at an unapproved time - regardless of detection, credentials, or false positives, which are separate concerns.$q$),
('cy-q-305','cysa',2,'2.1','mcq',2,$q$An e-commerce company that processes credit card payments must meet PCI DSS requirements for external vulnerability scanning. What does PCI DSS require?$q$,$q$["A one-time scan performed only at initial deployment","Quarterly external scans performed by an Approved Scanning Vendor (ASV), plus scans after significant network changes","Annual internal scans only, with no external requirement","A daily scan performed exclusively by internal staff"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$PCI DSS requires quarterly external vulnerability scans conducted specifically by a certified Approved Scanning Vendor, along with additional scanning after any significant change to the network - not a one-time, annual-only, or purely internal requirement.$q$),
('cy-q-306','cysa',2,'2.1','mcq',2,$q$Two systems in the same organization are scanned at very different frequencies: a public-facing payment gateway is scanned weekly, while an isolated internal reporting server is scanned twice a year. What best explains this difference?$q$,$q$["Scan cadence should be identical for every system for consistency","Scan frequency is typically risk-based, driven by factors like asset criticality, exposure, rate of change, and regulatory requirements","Only externally-visible systems can technically be scanned at all","Scan frequency depends solely on which operating system is installed"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Scan cadence is a risk-based decision - the payment gateway's high criticality, internet exposure, and likely regulatory scope justify frequent scanning, while a low-value, isolated, rarely-changing server does not need the same frequency.$q$),
('cy-q-307','cysa',2,'2.1','mcq',3,$q$A central vulnerability scanner deployed in the corporate data-center VLAN reports zero findings for a manufacturing OT segment known to run outdated software. What is the MOST likely explanation?$q$,$q$["The OT segment genuinely has no vulnerabilities","Network segmentation is preventing the central scanner from reaching hosts in the isolated OT segment, producing a blind spot rather than a clean result","The scanner is intentionally configured to ignore OT devices by design","OT devices cannot be vulnerable by definition"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Segmentation that isolates the OT network from the scanner's segment means the scanner simply can't reach those hosts - a zero-finding result here almost certainly reflects a coverage gap, not genuine security, especially given the segment's known outdated software.$q$),
('cy-q-308','cysa',2,'2.1','mcq',2,$q$A vulnerability management team schedules extra-cautious, closely supervised scanning windows for a segment hosting systems that store regulated healthcare records, compared to the standard scan process used elsewhere. What primarily justifies this extra caution?$q$,$q$["Regulated data increases both the impact of any scan-induced disruption and the sensitivity of the scan results themselves, which catalog exploitable weaknesses","Healthcare systems cannot be scanned under any circumstances","Sensitive data makes scanning entirely unnecessary","Extra caution is required only for cosmetic policy compliance with no real risk basis"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Systems holding regulated or highly sensitive data carry higher stakes on two fronts: disrupting them during a scan is costlier, and the resulting scan data is itself a sensitive map of exploitable weaknesses - both reasons for tighter scheduling and access controls around the process.$q$),
('cy-q-309','cysa',2,'2.1','ordering',2,$q$Order the following steps of standing up a new vulnerability scanning program, from FIRST to LAST.$q$,$q$["Perform asset discovery to build an accurate inventory of live hosts","Negotiate and document scan scope, authorized windows, and exclusions with stakeholders","Configure credentialed scan accounts and select applicable checks based on fingerprinted OS/services","Execute the scan and validate results before acting on findings"]$q$::jsonb,$q$[0,1,2,3]$q$::jsonb,$q$A sound program starts by discovering what actually exists, formalizes agreement on scope and timing before anything is probed, configures accurate credentialed/targeted checks based on what was fingerprinted, and only then executes and validates - each step depends on the one before it.$q$),
('cy-q-310','cysa',2,'2.1','matching',2,$q$Match each scanning concept to the description that best fits it.$q$,$q${"left":["Agent-based scanning","Agentless scanning","Credentialed scanning","Passive scanning"],"right":["Installed software reports posture continuously, even off the corporate network","A central scanner probes targets remotely with nothing installed on them","The scanner authenticates and inspects the system from the inside for accuracy","The scanner observes existing network traffic without directly probing targets"]}$q$::jsonb,$q$[0,1,2,3]$q$::jsonb,$q$Each scanning concept addresses a different constraint: agent-based scanning follows the endpoint anywhere, agentless scanning needs no installed software but requires network reachability, credentialed scanning trades access for accuracy, and passive scanning trades depth for zero probing risk.$q$),
('cy-q-311','cysa',2,'2.1','mcq',3,$q$A company's internal security team wants to perform its own PCI DSS-required quarterly external scan using an in-house scanner instead of a certified third party. Is this compliant?$q$,$q$["Yes, any internal scanner satisfies the requirement","No, PCI DSS requires the external scan specifically be performed by a PCI SSC-certified Approved Scanning Vendor (ASV)","Yes, as long as the scan runs from outside the corporate network","No, PCI DSS prohibits any form of external scanning entirely"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$PCI DSS is explicit that the quarterly external scan must be performed by a certified ASV, not merely run from an external vantage point by internal staff - the certification requirement is the point, not just the scan's network origin.$q$),
('cy-q-312','cysa',2,'2.1','mcq',1,$q$Which pair of standards are components of SCAP used respectively to describe machine-readable vulnerability/configuration state checks and to define structured, automatable security checklists?$q$,$q$["OVAL and XCCDF","CVSS and CVE","EPSS and KEV","SAST and DAST"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$OVAL defines machine-readable, testable definitions of system state such as vulnerabilities and configuration; XCCDF defines structured, automatable checklists and benchmarks. Both are SCAP components, distinct from scoring systems like CVSS/EPSS or testing methodologies like SAST/DAST.$q$),
('cy-q-313','cysa',2,'2.2','mcq',1,$q$A vulnerability scan report lists, for a single finding, the affected host's IP, a plugin/check ID, a CVSS score, a text description, and a remediation recommendation. What is the purpose of including all these fields together?$q$,$q$["To make the report longer for compliance purposes","To let an analyst triage the finding without needing to re-research it from scratch","Because the scanner is incapable of producing a shorter report","To satisfy a marketing requirement from the scanner vendor"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$A well-formed finding bundles everything an analyst needs to make a fast triage decision - what's affected, how severe, why, and what to do - so the analyst isn't forced to separately research the CVE or the affected asset before acting.$q$),
('cy-q-314','cysa',2,'2.2','mcq',2,$q$A scan of 300 servers reports 300 separate "critical" findings, all referencing the same CVE for an outdated library version. What is the MOST efficient next step?$q$,$q$["Treat each of the 300 findings as an independent remediation project","Group the findings by the shared CVE and apply one fix that resolves all 300 instances at once","Close all 300 findings as duplicates without further action","Escalate each of the 300 findings to a different remediation team"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Grouping findings by their shared root cause reveals that one patch or upgrade resolves all 300 instances, which is far more efficient than treating each host as its own unrelated project or, worse, dismissing them without action.$q$),
('cy-q-315','cysa',2,'2.2','mcq',2,$q$An analyst compares this month's scan results against last month's for the same subnet and finds 40 findings closed, 15 new findings, and 5 findings that have now appeared in four consecutive scans unresolved. What does the reappearing set of 5 findings indicate?$q$,$q$["The scanner is malfunctioning","Those findings are stalled in the remediation process and warrant escalation","Those findings were never real, so they should be dismissed as false positives","The scan schedule is running too frequently"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Findings that persist unresolved across multiple scan cycles indicate a stalled remediation, not a scanner problem or a false positive - this pattern is exactly what scan-to-scan trending is meant to surface so it can be escalated.$q$),
('cy-q-316','cysa',2,'2.2','mcq',2,$q$A finding reports a reflected input parameter on a specific URL that echoes unescaped user input back into the page, complete with the exact request used to trigger it. What TYPE of scanning tool most likely produced this output?$q$,$q$["An infrastructure/network vulnerability scanner","A web application scanner","A passive network traffic analyzer","A configuration compliance scanner"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Findings tied to a specific URL, parameter, and request/response evidence are characteristic of web application scanner output, which tests application-layer behavior rather than host-level ports and installed software.$q$),
('cy-q-317','cysa',2,'2.2','mcq',2,$q$A cloud security assessment tool flags a storage bucket configured with public read access and an IAM role attached to a virtual machine that grants far more permissions than the application actually uses. What category of tool produced this output?$q$,$q$["A traditional network port scanner","A cloud infrastructure assessment / posture management tool","A static application security testing tool","A physical security audit checklist"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Findings about misconfigured cloud resources - overly permissive bucket policies and excessive IAM permissions - are the defining output of a cloud infrastructure assessment/posture tool, distinct from network port scanning, source-code analysis, or physical audits.$q$),
('cy-q-318','cysa',2,'2.2','multi',2,$q$A scan report marks one finding "confirmed" (an exploit check succeeded) and a similar finding on another host "potential" (banner text suggested it but was not directly verified). Select the TWO correct interpretations. (Choose two.)$q$,$q$["The confirmed finding was actively verified and needs no further validation before prioritizing it","The potential finding should be manually confirmed before committing remediation resources to it, since it wasn't directly verified","Both findings carry identical certainty and should be treated as equally proven","'Potential' findings should always be deleted from the report without review"]$q$::jsonb,$q$[0,1]$q$::jsonb,$q$A confirmed finding has already cleared active verification and can be acted on directly, while a potential finding rests on an indirect signal and needs manual confirmation first - the two carry different certainty and should be treated differently, not identically or dismissed outright.$q$),
('cy-q-319','cysa',2,'2.2','mcq',3,$q$A team runs three different scanners - one uses a 1-5 severity scale, one uses Low/Medium/High/Critical labels, and one reports raw CVSS scores. Before comparing results across all three, what should the team do FIRST?$q$,$q$["Trust whichever tool's report arrived first","Normalize all findings to a common scale or format, such as CVSS, so severity is comparable across tools","Discard two of the three tools' reports arbitrarily","Average the three different scales together without any conversion"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Comparing severity across tools that use incompatible scales without normalizing first produces a meaningless comparison; converting everything to one common reference, such as CVSS, is the necessary first step before any cross-tool prioritization.$q$),
('cy-q-320','cysa',2,'2.2','mcq',1,$q$Which type of vulnerability-assessment tool output would primarily reference host IP addresses, open ports, and installed software versions rather than URLs and request parameters?$q$,$q$["An infrastructure/network vulnerability scanner","A web application scanner","A source-code static analyzer","A social engineering test report"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Infrastructure/network scanners assess hosts, ports, and installed software directly, in contrast to web application scanners, which report findings tied to URLs and parameters, or code/social-engineering assessments, which produce entirely different artifacts.$q$),
('cy-q-321','cysa',2,'2.2','ordering',2,$q$Order the steps an analyst should take, FIRST to LAST, when a new vulnerability scan report is delivered.$q$,$q$["Normalize findings from the tool(s) used into a common severity scale","Group related findings by shared CVE/plugin ID across affected hosts","Distinguish confirmed findings from unverified/potential findings needing manual review","Compare against the prior scan to identify new, resolved, and recurring findings"]$q$::jsonb,$q$[0,1,2,3]$q$::jsonb,$q$Effective analysis of scan output starts by making findings comparable (normalizing), then consolidates related findings (grouping), then separates what's proven from what still needs confirmation, and finally places the results in context against prior scans to see real trend and progress.$q$),
('cy-q-322','cysa',2,'2.2','matching',2,$q$Match each tool type to the output characteristic it typically produces.$q$,$q${"left":["Web application scanner","Cloud infrastructure assessment tool","Infrastructure/network scanner","Compliance/configuration scanner"],"right":["Findings tied to a URL/parameter with a request/response proof of concept","Findings describing misconfigured cloud resources, like an overly permissive storage policy","Findings tied to a host IP, open port, and installed service version","Findings tied to a specific benchmark or compliance control failing on a host"]}$q$::jsonb,$q$[0,1,2,3]$q$::jsonb,$q$Each tool class inspects a different layer and reports accordingly: web scanners target the application layer, cloud posture tools target resource configuration, network scanners target host/service exposure, and compliance scanners target adherence to a defined benchmark.$q$),
('cy-q-323','cysa',2,'2.2','mcq',3,$q$A security team discovers, via an external attack-surface tool, several forgotten subdomains and a staging server exposed to the internet that never appeared in the official asset inventory. What does this scenario best illustrate the value of?$q$,$q$["Internal credentialed scanning alone is always sufficient","External attack surface management (EASM), which continuously discovers internet-facing assets from an outsider's perspective, including ones missing from official inventories","Static code analysis of the company's applications","Physical penetration testing of office badge readers"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$This is exactly the gap EASM tools are built to close: discovering internet-facing assets - forgotten subdomains, shadow IT, unlisted staging servers - that never made it into the official inventory and that internal-only scanning would never see.$q$),
('cy-q-324','cysa',2,'2.3','mcq',1,$q$Beyond a vulnerability's CVSS score, which combination of factors should drive real-world remediation priority?$q$,$q$["CVSS score alone is always sufficient","Asset criticality, exposure to attack, real-world exploitability, and the value at risk if the asset were compromised","The alphabetical order of the CVE identifier","Only the age of the finding, regardless of any other factor"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$CVSS captures intrinsic technical severity but says nothing about your specific exposure, the criticality of the affected asset, or whether attackers are actually exploiting it - real prioritization layers all of these together.$q$),
('cy-q-325','cysa',2,'2.3','mcq',2,$q$A vulnerability has had a proof-of-concept exploit available for months with no significant change in exploitation activity. This week, a ready-to-use exploit module is added to a widely used penetration-testing framework. How should this change prioritization?$q$,$q$["No change - a proof-of-concept and a weaponized exploit are equivalent risk","Priority should increase sharply, since a weaponized, ready-to-use exploit dramatically lowers the skill needed to exploit it at scale","Priority should decrease, since the exploit is now public and 'already known'","This information is irrelevant to prioritization decisions"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Weaponization is a meaningful inflection point: once an exploit is packaged into ready-to-use tooling, far less skill is needed to use it at scale, sharply raising practical exploitation likelihood even if the CVSS score never changes.$q$),
('cy-q-326','cysa',2,'2.3','mcq',2,$q$Organization A treats any CVSS 7+ finding as an emergency requiring same-week remediation. Organization B allows 90 days for CVSS 7+ findings on non-critical systems. What MOST directly explains this difference?$q$,$q$["Organization B is simply non-compliant with a universal remediation standard","The organizations have different risk appetites, which set their own acceptable prioritization thresholds and SLAs","CVSS scores are calculated differently at each organization","Organization A does not actually use CVSS scoring"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$There is no single universal remediation deadline tied to a CVSS score; each organization's risk appetite determines how aggressive its own SLA thresholds are, which is why two organizations can legitimately set very different timelines for the same score.$q$),
('cy-q-327','cysa',2,'2.3','mcq',2,$q$A vulnerability's CVSS score has not changed, but a working exploit for it was just published on a public repository. What should happen to its real-world prioritization?$q$,$q$["It should stay exactly the same since CVSS hasn't changed","It should rise, since public exploit availability increases practical exploitation likelihood even though CVSS, an intrinsic severity measure, is unaffected","It should decrease, since public exploits mean the flaw is now well understood and therefore safe","CVSS scores are automatically recalculated the instant an exploit is published"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$CVSS base score reflects intrinsic severity and doesn't move on its own when an exploit is published, but practical risk clearly does - public exploit code is a strong signal that should push real-world prioritization upward independent of the static score.$q$),
('cy-q-328','cysa',2,'2.3','mcq',2,$q$When estimating the "value at risk" for a finding on a customer-facing payment system, which two dimensions are typically combined?$q$,$q$["CPU utilization and disk space","Financial impact (direct loss, fines, recovery cost) and reputational impact (customer trust, brand damage)","The vendor's stock price and the CVE publication date","Network bandwidth and scan duration"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Value at risk is a business-impact estimate, combining what a successful exploit would directly cost financially with the reputational damage of the incident becoming known - neither purely technical metrics nor unrelated figures capture this.$q$),
('cy-q-329','cysa',2,'2.3','mcq',3,$q$A company's cyber insurance policy requires all critical vulnerabilities on internet-facing systems to be patched within 15 days as a condition of coverage. How should this affect the company's vulnerability prioritization program?$q$,$q$["It should have no effect, since insurance terms are unrelated to security operations","It should be incorporated as an explicit prioritization driver, since failing the SLA could jeopardize insurance coverage after a future incident","It should completely replace CVSS scoring in the program","It only affects the finance department, not the security team"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Cyber insurance requirements are a real, binding prioritization input: missing the insurer's patch timeline can mean a denied claim or lost coverage precisely when it's needed most, so security teams need to fold that SLA into their prioritization decisions.$q$),
('cy-q-330','cysa',2,'2.3','mcq',3,$q$Instead of ranking every finding purely by a numeric severity score, a team adopts a decision-tree framework that asks whether a vulnerability is under active exploitation, whether the affected system is exposed, and how a compromise would affect the mission - producing an "act now" or "schedule normally" recommendation. Which approach is this?$q$,$q$["CVSS Environmental scoring","SSVC (Stakeholder-Specific Vulnerability Categorization)","EPSS","A plain CVE lookup"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$SSVC's defining feature is exactly this: a structured decision tree over factors like exploitation status, exposure, and mission impact, producing an actionable recommendation rather than only a numeric score.$q$),
('cy-q-331','cysa',2,'2.3','mcq',2,$q$A vulnerability with no known public exploit code sits on an internet-facing server that processes regulated financial transactions. Why might a risk-based program still prioritize it highly?$q$,$q$["It shouldn't - without a known exploit, it's automatically low priority","Exposure, asset criticality, and potential impact alone can justify urgent treatment, rather than waiting for an exploit to appear first","CVSS automatically assumes an exploit exists for every vulnerability","Financial systems are exempt from standard vulnerability prioritization"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Waiting for a public exploit before treating a high-exposure, high-criticality finding as urgent is a reactive posture, not a risk-based one - exposure and potential impact alone are legitimate grounds for prioritizing it now.$q$),
('cy-q-332','cysa',2,'2.3','mcq',1,$q$What is the primary purpose of vulnerability triage, as distinct from remediation?$q$,$q$["Triage analyzes and ranks newly found vulnerabilities by risk to decide what gets addressed first; remediation is the actual fix applied afterward","Triage and remediation describe the same activity performed twice","Triage only applies to findings later determined to be false positives","Remediation always happens before triage in a well-run program"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Triage is the analysis and ranking step that determines what gets worked on and in what order; remediation is the downstream action of actually fixing the prioritized findings - triage necessarily precedes it, not the reverse.$q$),
('cy-q-333','cysa',2,'2.3','multi',2,$q$Select the TWO factors that legitimately belong in a risk-based vulnerability prioritization decision, beyond the raw CVSS base score. (Choose two.)$q$,$q$["Whether the vulnerability is listed as actively exploited or has a high exploitation probability score","The criticality and exposure of the specific asset affected","The order in which findings happen to appear alphabetically in the report","The number of characters in the CVE identifier"]$q$::jsonb,$q$[0,1]$q$::jsonb,$q$Real-world prioritization draws on exploitation signals and asset context - both meaningfully change risk - while alphabetical ordering and identifier length carry no risk information whatsoever.$q$),
('cy-q-334','cysa',2,'2.3','ordering',2,$q$Order the following steps of a risk-based vulnerability prioritization workflow, from FIRST to LAST.$q$,$q$["Gather the vulnerability's technical severity, such as a CVSS base score","Layer in real-world exploitability signals, such as active-exploitation status and public exploit availability","Factor in the affected asset's exposure and business criticality (value at risk)","Assign a final priority tier and remediation deadline consistent with organizational risk appetite"]$q$::jsonb,$q$[0,1,2,3]$q$::jsonb,$q$A sound prioritization workflow starts from a baseline technical severity, layers on how likely exploitation actually is right now, adds the specific asset's context and value, and only then converts all of that into a concrete priority and deadline aligned with the organization's risk tolerance.$q$),
('cy-q-335','cysa',2,'2.3','matching',2,$q$Match each prioritization factor to the example that best represents it.$q$,$q${"left":["Exploitability","Exposure","Asset criticality","Organizational risk appetite"],"right":["A weaponized exploit module now exists in a public penetration-testing framework","The affected server is reachable directly from the internet","The affected database stores the company's entire customer payment history","The company enforces a strict 72-hour remediation SLA for any critical finding"]}$q$::jsonb,$q$[0,1,2,3]$q$::jsonb,$q$Each factor answers a different question in prioritization: exploitability asks how usable an exploit currently is, exposure asks how reachable the target is, asset criticality asks how much is at stake, and risk appetite asks how aggressively the organization has chosen to respond.$q$),
('cy-q-336','cysa',2,'2.3','mcq',3,$q$A risk team calculates that a specific unpatched vulnerability, if exploited, would cost approximately $200,000 per incident, and estimates a 25% chance of exploitation in a given year. What figure are they calculating, and what is it useful for?$q$,$q$["Annualized Loss Expectancy (ALE), useful for weighing the financial value at risk when prioritizing remediation spend","CVSS Environmental score, useful for technical severity adjustment","EPSS, useful for measuring source code quality","Mean Time To Remediate, useful for SLA tracking"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Multiplying the cost of a single occurrence by its estimated annual rate of occurrence produces Annualized Loss Expectancy, a financial figure used to weigh how much value is genuinely at risk when deciding where to spend remediation effort.$q$),
('cy-q-337','cysa',2,'2.4','mcq',1,$q$A login form rejects any input containing unexpected characters or exceeding the expected field length before passing it to the application logic. What control is this, and what class of attack does it primarily reduce?$q$,$q$["Output encoding, reducing cross-site scripting","Input validation, reducing injection-style attacks by rejecting malformed data at the boundary","Network segmentation, reducing lateral movement","Patch management, reducing known-CVE exploitation"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Rejecting data that doesn't match the expected type, length, or format before it reaches application logic is input validation, and it primarily closes off the entry point that injection-style attacks rely on.$q$),
('cy-q-338','cysa',2,'2.4','mcq',2,$q$A developer rewrites database queries to use parameterized statements instead of concatenating raw user input into SQL strings. Why does this stop SQL injection?$q$,$q$["It encrypts the database connection","It separates the query structure from user-supplied data, so attacker input is always treated as data and never as executable SQL syntax","It blocks all traffic originating from unknown IP addresses","It removes the need for a database entirely"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Parameterized queries fix the actual mechanism SQL injection relies on: the query's command structure is fixed ahead of time, and user input is only ever plugged in as data afterward, so it can never be reinterpreted as SQL syntax.$q$),
('cy-q-339','cysa',2,'2.4','mcq',2,$q$A web application escapes special HTML characters in user-submitted comments before displaying them back to other visitors. Which vulnerability class does this control primarily address?$q$,$q$["SQL injection","Cross-site scripting (XSS)","Buffer overflow","Directory traversal"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Escaping special characters before rendering user-supplied content back to a browser is output encoding, the primary defense against cross-site scripting, since it renders any injected script as inert text instead of executable code.$q$),
('cy-q-340','cysa',2,'2.4','mcq',3,$q$A C application copies user input into a fixed-length stack buffer without checking the input's length, allowing an attacker to overwrite adjacent memory. Which combination of controls specifically mitigates this class of vulnerability?$q$,$q$["Output encoding and a content security policy","Bounds checking, safe string-handling functions, and OS protections like stack canaries and ASLR","Parameterized queries and stored procedures","Anti-CSRF tokens and same-site cookies"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Buffer overflows are memory-safety issues, mitigated at the code level by bounds checking and safe string handling, and at the OS level by protections like stack canaries and address space layout randomization - none of the web-focused controls in the other options apply here.$q$),
('cy-q-341','cysa',2,'2.4','mcq',3,$q$An attacker frees a memory object, then triggers the application to reuse a dangling pointer to that same, now-reallocated memory, allowing them to influence what the pointer actually references. What vulnerability class is this?$q$,$q$["SQL injection","Use-after-free","Cross-site request forgery","Directory traversal"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Continuing to use a pointer to memory that has already been freed, which an attacker can then reallocate with their own data, is the defining pattern of a use-after-free vulnerability, distinct from the web-focused options listed.$q$),
('cy-q-342','cysa',2,'2.4','mcq',2,$q$An application passes unsanitized user input directly to a system shell command, letting an attacker append a destructive command to the input. Which vulnerability class does this represent, and how does its root cause compare to SQL injection?$q$,$q$["Command injection - unlike SQL injection, unsanitized input never reaches an interpreter in this case","Command injection - like SQL injection, the root cause is unsanitized input reaching an interpreter, here the OS shell instead of a database","Cross-site scripting, unrelated to SQL injection","Buffer overflow, unrelated to SQL injection"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Command injection and SQL injection share the same underlying pattern - unsanitized input reaching an interpreter that executes it as commands - the only difference is which interpreter (an OS shell versus a database engine) is on the receiving end.$q$),
('cy-q-343','cysa',2,'2.4','multi',2,$q$Select the TWO findings that would correctly be classified as cryptographic-failure vulnerabilities during a code/control review. (Choose two.)$q$,$q$["A web application still supports the deprecated MD5 hash for password storage","Sensitive customer data is transmitted in plaintext over an unencrypted connection","A login form limits input length to 64 characters","A firewall rule blocks inbound traffic on an unused port"]$q$::jsonb,$q$[0,1]$q$::jsonb,$q$Using a deprecated, broken hash algorithm and transmitting sensitive data without encryption are both cryptographic failures - they undermine the confidentiality or integrity protection cryptography is meant to provide. Input length limits and firewall rules are unrelated controls.$q$),
('cy-q-344','cysa',2,'2.4','mcq',2,$q$An application has no account lockout policy and issues predictable, sequential session tokens after login. Which set of controls would MOST directly address these broken-authentication weaknesses?$q$,$q$["Multi-factor authentication, rate limiting/lockout thresholds, and cryptographically random session tokens","Output encoding and a content security policy","Bounds checking and safe memory-handling functions","Network segmentation applied on its own"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Broken-authentication weaknesses like missing lockout and predictable tokens are addressed directly by MFA, rate limiting/lockout policy, and strong, random session token generation - not by controls aimed at entirely different vulnerability classes.$q$),
('cy-q-345','cysa',2,'2.4','mcq',1,$q$A security team places an inline control in front of a web application that inspects incoming HTTP requests against rulesets for known SQL injection and XSS patterns, blocking matches before they reach the app. What is this control?$q$,$q$["A Web Application Firewall (WAF)","A vulnerability scanner","A SIEM","A patch management system"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Inline HTTP(S) traffic inspection against attack-pattern rulesets, blocking matches before they reach the application, is exactly the function of a Web Application Firewall.$q$),
('cy-q-346','cysa',2,'2.4','mcq',2,$q$A web application has a known SQL injection flaw that cannot be patched for several weeks due to a vendor dependency. The team deploys a WAF rule to block the specific injection pattern in the meantime. What is the WAF acting as in this scenario?$q$,$q$["A permanent remediation that eliminates the underlying flaw","A compensating control that reduces exposure while the actual code-level fix is pending","A network segmentation control","An input validation library embedded directly in the application code"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$The WAF rule doesn't touch the flawed code itself - it filters traffic in front of it - making it a compensating control that reduces exposure temporarily while the real fix is still pending, not a permanent remediation.$q$),
('cy-q-347','cysa',2,'2.4','mcq',2,$q$A web application process runs under a service account with read-only access to only the specific database tables it needs, rather than full administrative database rights. If an attacker exploits a vulnerability in this application, what does this configuration limit?$q$,$q$["It has no effect on the impact of a successful exploit","It limits the blast radius of a successful exploit to what that narrow set of privileges can reach, under the principle of least privilege","It prevents the underlying vulnerability from being exploited at all","It only affects vulnerability scan accuracy, not exploit impact"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Least privilege doesn't stop a vulnerability from being exploited, but it caps the damage: an attacker who compromises this process only inherits the narrow, read-only access it was granted, not full database control.$q$),
('cy-q-348','cysa',2,'2.4','mcq',2,$q$Which statement correctly contrasts secure-coding training with a WAF as controls against injection vulnerabilities?$q$,$q$["Secure-coding training is a preventive control that stops the flaw from being written into the code; a WAF is a compensating/detective control that filters traffic after the flawed code already exists","A WAF is always a substitute for secure coding, and no training is needed once one is deployed","Secure-coding training and a WAF address entirely unrelated vulnerability classes","Both controls operate at exactly the same layer of the application stack"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Secure-coding training acts before the flaw ever exists, preventing it at the source; a WAF acts afterward, filtering traffic around code that may still contain the underlying flaw - they operate at different points in the defense chain and complement rather than replace each other.$q$),
('cy-q-349','cysa',2,'2.4','mcq',3,$q$A control embedded inside a running application observes its actual internal execution context - which function is about to run with which data - and blocks an attack at that moment, rather than only inspecting network traffic from outside the application. What is this control called, and how does it differ from a WAF?$q$,$q$["RASP (Runtime Application Self-Protection); unlike a WAF, it has visibility into the application's real internal execution context, not just external traffic","A WAF; it is functionally identical to RASP in every respect","SAST; it only analyzes source code before deployment","IAST; it only runs during pre-production testing"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$RASP runs inside the application itself and can react to what the application is actually about to do internally, giving it context a WAF - which only inspects external network traffic - simply doesn't have.$q$),
('cy-q-350','cysa',2,'2.4','mcq',2,$q$Before deploying new servers, an organization applies a documented, vetted set of secure configuration settings - disabling unnecessary services, enforcing strong password policy defaults - based on a published benchmark. What is this practice called, and what does it reduce?$q$,$q$["Hardening using a baseline, such as a CIS Benchmark, reducing the system's attack surface from insecure default settings","Vulnerability scanning, reducing the number of scans required going forward","Risk acceptance, reducing documentation overhead","Patch management, reducing the CVE backlog directly"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Applying a vetted, published set of secure configuration settings before deployment is hardening against a baseline - it shrinks the attack surface by removing unnecessary services and insecure defaults from the start, rather than reacting to findings later.$q$),
('cy-q-351','cysa',2,'2.4','mcq',2,$q$A legacy application with a known unpatched vulnerability cannot be taken offline. The team isolates it onto its own VLAN with strict firewall rules limiting which systems can reach it. What does this achieve?$q$,$q$["It fully remediates the underlying vulnerability","It acts as a compensating control, reducing the practical blast radius and reachable attack paths without fixing the flaw itself","It has no security benefit since the vulnerability still technically exists","It automatically patches the application over time"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Segmentation doesn't touch the flaw itself, but it meaningfully reduces what could reach the vulnerable system and what an attacker could pivot to afterward - a real risk reduction even though the underlying vulnerability remains unpatched.$q$),
('cy-q-352','cysa',2,'2.4','multi',2,$q$Select the TWO controls that directly help prevent injection-style vulnerabilities, such as SQL injection and command injection, from being exploitable in application code. (Choose two.)$q$,$q$["Parameterized queries / prepared statements","Strict input validation at the point data enters the application","Enabling verbose error messages in production for easier debugging","Increasing the session timeout duration"]$q$::jsonb,$q$[0,1]$q$::jsonb,$q$Parameterized queries and strict input validation both address the actual mechanism injection attacks exploit. Verbose production error messages can leak useful information to an attacker rather than prevent injection, and session timeout duration is unrelated to injection vulnerabilities.$q$),
('cy-q-353','cysa',2,'2.4','ordering',2,$q$Order these controls against a web application injection vulnerability from MOST preventive (addressing the root cause) to MOST compensating (reducing impact without fixing the cause).$q$,$q$["Secure coding training that stops developers from introducing the flaw","Parameterized queries implemented in the vulnerable code itself","A WAF rule blocking the specific known injection pattern in production","Network segmentation limiting which systems can reach the vulnerable application"]$q$::jsonb,$q$[0,1,2,3]$q$::jsonb,$q$Preventive controls stop the flaw from ever existing (training, then the actual code fix); as you move toward compensating controls, the flaw itself remains but its exposure and exploitability are reduced from further out - a WAF filters the specific pattern, and segmentation limits reachability entirely independent of the application layer.$q$),
('cy-q-354','cysa',2,'2.4','matching',2,$q$Match each vulnerability class to its primary mitigating control.$q$,$q${"left":["SQL injection","Cross-site scripting","Buffer overflow","Cross-site request forgery"],"right":["Parameterized queries / prepared statements","Output encoding of data rendered back to the browser","Bounds checking and memory-safe handling of fixed-size buffers","Anti-CSRF tokens and same-site cookie attributes"]}$q$::jsonb,$q$[0,1,2,3]$q$::jsonb,$q$Each vulnerability class has a control that addresses its specific root mechanism: SQL injection is fixed by separating query structure from data, XSS by encoding output, buffer overflow by memory-safe handling, and CSRF by tokens/cookie attributes that prove request origin.$q$),
('cy-q-355','cysa',2,'2.5','mcq',1,$q$A newly hired analyst asks where remediation SLA tiers, escalation triggers, and the exception-approval process are documented for their organization. Where should this information live?$q$,$q$["Nowhere - these are decided ad hoc each time a finding appears","The organization's vulnerability management policy, which governs the program at large","Only in the scanner's default configuration file","In each individual scan report, repeated every time"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Program-level governance elements like SLA tiers, escalation triggers, and the exception process belong in the organization's vulnerability management policy - the document that defines how the whole program operates, not in ad hoc decisions or a single scan report.$q$),
('cy-q-356','cysa',2,'2.5','ordering',2,$q$Order the typical patch management pipeline steps, from FIRST to LAST, before a fix reaches production.$q$,$q$["Test the patch in a non-production/staging environment","Schedule the deployment through change control","Apply the patch to production during the approved window","Validate success post-deployment, with rollback ready if needed"]$q$::jsonb,$q$[0,1,2,3]$q$::jsonb,$q$A disciplined patch pipeline tests a fix before it ever touches production, gets the deployment formally scheduled and approved, applies it during the agreed window, and then validates the outcome with a rollback option ready in case something goes wrong.$q$),
('cy-q-357','cysa',2,'2.5','mcq',2,$q$A team approves a formal exception allowing a finding to remain unremediated, with compensating monitoring in place, but sets no expiration or review date on the exception. What risk does this create?$q$,$q$["No risk - exceptions are meant to be permanent once approved","The exception can silently become permanent even after the circumstances that justified it change, without ever being reassessed","It automatically expires after 24 hours regardless of what was documented","It automatically converts the finding into a false positive"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Without a review date, an exception has no forcing function to revisit it - conditions that justified it originally can change entirely while the exception quietly stays in place, unexamined, indefinitely.$q$),
('cy-q-358','cysa',2,'2.5','mcq',2,$q$A critical finding on a crown-jewel asset has now exceeded its remediation SLA by two weeks with no progress. What should happen according to a well-defined vulnerability management program?$q$,$q$["Nothing further - the SLA breach reporting alone is sufficient action","The defined escalation procedure should trigger, notifying the specified chain of stakeholders to intervene","The finding should simply be closed without remediation","The scan should just be re-run more often until the finding disappears"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$An SLA breach on a high-criticality finding is precisely the trigger condition an escalation procedure exists for - it's meant to actively pull in the right people to intervene, not just be logged in a report and left alone.$q$),
('cy-q-359','cysa',2,'2.5','mcq',2,$q$A researcher privately reports a critical flaw to a software vendor and agrees to hold public disclosure for 90 days while the vendor develops a fix. What is this practice called, and what does the 90-day window accomplish?$q$,$q$["Full disclosure; it has no defined purpose beyond delay","Coordinated Vulnerability Disclosure (CVD); the embargo period gives the vendor time to develop and ship a fix before technical details become public","A bug bounty; the window sets the payout amount owed to the researcher","Zero-day marketing; the window is meant to maximize the exploit's resale value"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$This is Coordinated Vulnerability Disclosure - the researcher's report stays private for an agreed embargo window specifically so the vendor can build and release a fix before the technical details reach the public and, by extension, potential attackers.$q$),
('cy-q-360','cysa',2,'2.5','mcq',1,$q$A vendor maintains a dedicated internal team that receives external vulnerability reports about its own products, triages them, and coordinates the fix and disclosure timeline with the reporting researcher. What is this team called?$q$,$q$["A PSIRT (Product Security Incident Response Team)","A SOC","A red team","A change advisory board"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$A PSIRT is a vendor's dedicated function for receiving, triaging, and coordinating the response to vulnerabilities reported in that vendor's own products - distinct from a SOC (monitors an organization's own environment), a red team (simulates attacks), or a change advisory board (approves changes).$q$),
('cy-q-361','cysa',2,'2.5','mcq',2,$q$A scan repeatedly flags new vulnerabilities in a critical application running on an operating system that reached end-of-life two years ago and no longer receives vendor patches. What does this situation require, beyond simply logging each new finding?$q$,$q$["Nothing beyond documenting each new finding as it appears","An ongoing management plan - compensating controls like isolation and monitoring, and a migration timeline - since the vendor will never patch this platform again","Waiting indefinitely for the vendor to resume support","Disabling the vulnerability scanner for that specific system"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Since the vendor will never issue another patch for an end-of-life platform, every future finding on it is permanently unfixable at the source - the only real response is an ongoing plan of compensating controls and an eventual migration, not repeated logging.$q$),
('cy-q-362','cysa',2,'2.5','mcq',2,$q$A cloud-native environment where resources are created and destroyed within minutes finds that a monthly vulnerability scan schedule consistently misses short-lived, vulnerable resources. What approach better fits this environment?$q$,$q$["Continuous vulnerability management integrated with near-real-time asset discovery, rather than a periodic point-in-time scan schedule","Switching to an annual scan schedule instead of monthly","Scanning only during business hours going forward","Removing vulnerability management entirely for cloud assets"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Periodic, point-in-time scanning structurally cannot keep pace with resources that exist for only minutes; continuous, near-real-time discovery and assessment integrated into daily operations is what's actually needed to cover ephemeral cloud environments.$q$),
('cy-q-363','cysa',2,'2.5','mcq',2,$q$A scan reports a critical finding on a host that isn't listed in the organization's asset inventory, and no one can immediately identify who owns it or how critical it is. What does this delay illustrate?$q$,$q$["Asset inventory has no real bearing on vulnerability response","An accurate, current asset inventory is a prerequisite for prioritizing and acting on findings - without it, even a critical finding can stall","The finding should be automatically dismissed as a false positive","Only externally-facing assets need to be inventoried at all"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Without knowing what an asset is, who owns it, and how critical it is, a team can't meaningfully prioritize or act on a finding - this scenario shows exactly why current asset inventory is a foundational input to vulnerability response, not an optional nicety.$q$),
('cy-q-364','cysa',2,'2.5','mcq',3,$q$The same category of missing-patch finding keeps appearing on newly provisioned servers month after month, despite each individual finding being remediated when found. What should the team do to actually stop the pattern?$q$,$q$["Keep remediating each new instance individually forever, with no other change","Perform root cause analysis to find the underlying process gap, such as a base image missing the patch, so newly provisioned servers stop being born vulnerable","Increase scan frequency without changing anything else about the process","Formally accept the risk permanently since it keeps recurring"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Fixing each instance without asking why the same category keeps appearing just treats the symptom; root cause analysis finds the actual process gap - like a stale base image - so future servers stop inheriting the same flaw in the first place.$q$),
('cy-q-365','cysa',2,'2.5','mcq',3,$q$A critical, actively-exploited vulnerability needs to be patched immediately, but the organization's standard change management process requires two weeks of testing and approval before any production change. What is the appropriate resolution?$q$,$q$["Skip change management entirely and patch without any process at all","Wait the full standard two weeks regardless of active exploitation","Use an expedited/emergency-change path designed for urgent, high-risk situations, balancing speed with appropriate oversight","Permanently disable change management for all future patches going forward"]$q$::jsonb,'2'::jsonb,$q$Mature programs resolve this exact tension with a defined emergency/expedited change path - it preserves essential oversight while allowing urgent, high-risk fixes to move far faster than the standard two-week process, rather than abandoning change control entirely or ignoring active exploitation.$q$),
('cy-q-366','cysa',2,'2.5','mcq',2,$q$One team measures remediation SLA compliance from the moment a finding is discovered by a scan; another measures it from the moment the fix is validated as deployed. Comparing their SLA compliance percentages directly leads to what problem?$q$,$q$["No problem - both measurements are equivalent regardless of starting point","A misleading comparison, since the two teams are measuring the SLA clock from different starting points, so their percentages aren't actually comparable","It always favors whichever team uses the later starting point, with no exceptions","It has no bearing on program metrics either way"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$If the SLA clock starts at different points - discovery versus fix validation - the resulting percentages measure different things entirely, making a direct comparison misleading unless both teams define the starting point consistently first.$q$),
('cy-q-367','cysa',2,'2.5','multi',2,$q$Select the TWO practices that are characteristic of a mature, well-governed vulnerability response process. (Choose two.)$q$,$q$["A documented exception process with mandatory expiration and periodic review dates","A defined escalation path triggered when SLA thresholds are breached","Leaving accepted risks undocumented so they don't clutter the risk register","Allowing every exception to remain in place indefinitely without ever revisiting it"]$q$::jsonb,$q$[0,1]$q$::jsonb,$q$Mature programs document exceptions with real expiration and review requirements, and define clear escalation triggers for SLA breaches - the opposite of leaving risk acceptance undocumented or letting exceptions sit unreviewed forever, both of which erode program integrity.$q$),
('cy-q-368','cysa',2,'2.5','matching',2,$q$Match each vulnerability response term to its function.$q$,$q${"left":["PSIRT","CVD embargo period","Escalation procedure","Root cause analysis"],"right":["A vendor's internal team coordinating fixes and disclosure timelines for reported flaws","The window during which a reported flaw's details are held before public disclosure","The trigger and notification chain used when normal remediation isn't keeping pace","Investigating why a vulnerability existed, to prevent the same category from recurring"]}$q$::jsonb,$q$[0,1,2,3]$q$::jsonb,$q$Each term plays a distinct role in the response and management process: the PSIRT is the vendor-side coordinating function, the embargo period buys time before disclosure, escalation procedures handle stalled remediation, and root cause analysis addresses recurrence at its source.$q$);
